
  [1mThird fork: what does "care about" actually mean?[0m

  This is the heart of your request and it determines the stored subscription format — the thing most expensive to
  change later, because existing records have to be migrated when the states change.

  - [1mImplicit only[0m — you're subscribed to tasks you created or are assigned to. Buys zero UI and zero user effort; it
    just works. Costs: you can't follow a task you're merely interested in, and you can't mute a noisy one you're
    assigned to. Stored as nothing at all — subscription is derived from the task's own fields, which is why it's the
    cheapest to start and the most annoying to outgrow.
  - [1mExplicit only[0m — a follow/star toggle per task. Buys total user control and a trivial store (a set of task IDs).
    Costs: nothing is ever notified by default, so the feature looks broken until someone manually follows things. Bad
    first-run experience.
  - [1mImplicit with explicit override[0m — auto-subscribed when you create or are assigned a task, plus a follow/mute toggle
    that wins over the default. Buys sensible defaults [3mand[0m control. Costs: the record needs three states ([38;5;153mfollowed[39m,
    [38;5;153mmuted[39m, absent-means-derive), and every read has to resolve override-then-default rather than just looking up a flag.

  [1mMy recommendation: implicit with explicit override.[0m It's what GitHub, Linear, and Jira all converged on, and the
  reason is that the other two each fail at one end — implicit-only can't be silenced, explicit-only starts silent. The
  extra cost is one small resolution function, and getting the three-state record right now is much cheaper than
  migrating a two-state one later.
